 responsible for it him  the nurses the  hospital administration or the system  that requires that to happen  in order to resolve it  the answer would have to be that  somebody has to say well  yes i was too tired  yes i did make a mistake that's the  starting point isn't it  exactly i couldn't agree with you more  and that is uh that is why i think we  are  running serious risks with patient  safety having very stretched staff at  the moment under enormous pressure i  think the chances of those kinds of  mistakes happening  are much greater because of the the  pressures that staff are under  um there's another difference with the  airline industry which i i think i only  really came to understand late in my  time as health secretary which is that  if you discover that the  you know the right wing nut on the  left-hand engine of this type of plane  um  needs to be uh  checked every time the plane takes off  or something like that  and if you just if you say if you don't  do this  there's a risk of that engine  failing  you just need to issue a simple  instruction like that and every airline  all around the world will immediately  make sure they check that wind nut every  time they take off and you don't need to  worry about implementation because the  airlines it's mission critical not to  have a crash  but if you um  have a mistake that's made for example  when a baby is being born  but in a certain situation you need to  use  you know this instrument or that  instrument whatever  it's not the case that just sending that  out as an instruction means that every  maternity department in the country will  immediately follow that  because they are already receiving  hundreds of instructions every single  day  on different issues  and they're also extremely busy  delivering babies  and so when it comes to healthcare  systems you have to spend a lot more  time working out how you're going to get  the bandwidth of the people who are  doing these things to change the system  because it won't happen automatically so  there's a  much more thought needs to be given to  implementation  the the the changes which uh you've made  in response to the various  inquiries that the new changes you  described  have you had a system of audit to check  what effect they've actually had  um  there are ways that we can check that  have you yes um and has it yes uh i  would i would say so but i'm a  politician and i'm proud of my record in  that respect but for example  um  following the francis inquiry we  introduced the ofsted system so we  didn't know which hospitals were good  and which hospitals weren't by the time  i left office  three million more patients were using  good or outstanding hospitals than when  i arrived it's now four million more  patients using good or outstanding  hospitals so i think there was  progress and i do think um the nhs is  much more transparent when i  listened to sajid javi giving his  response to the ochentan  report  and being completely honest about the  most terrible failings in nhs care in  shoes print alfred  i thought that that is a very big change  i was the first health secretary to  stand at the dispatch box  and talk in very graphic terms about nhs  failures and now here's someone two  health secretaries on using exactly the  same language so i think  there has been change i think the nhs is  is more compassionate but i wouldn't  want to pretend that there's not a long  way to go  um  same sort of area in one sense is  dealing with the the question of how we  we challenge  what has been received wisdom and may be  wrong  um one of the  problems which  has been discussed already uh in this  inquiry  uh has been the the problem that the  civil servants may not stay although  they stay in post for longer than junior  ministers  um something which you've already said  you find  uncomfortable as a way of having a  good  or better government  but but they do leave and they leave  perhaps more often than they used