 but the  truth was  um we there was group think that if  there was a pandemic  it would be a flu pandemic because  that's what we'd had before and we  hadn't given enough thought to the fact  that it might be a different type of  pandemic  so exercise cygnus which employed which  involved hundreds of civil servants and  me as the secretary of state at the time  you can look through the whole report  and testing is not mentioned once there  is no mention of testing at all that  turned out to be our biggest problem  when we actually had  that pandemic  and so i feel that  it's about there was a group think that  said if we have a pandemic in the future  it'll be similar to the pandemics we  have in the past and i think  that  what one needs to do is to put in place  people whose job it is to challenge  groupthink  at every stage and make sure that and  it's it's particularly challenging in  academia and in the civil service  because they're very consensual places  where people don't like to upset each  other everyone likes to try and find a  solution that everyone's happy with and  people don't like flies in the ointment  but actually you do need that you need  people who are going to challenge and  and it can't just be ministers i think  that's the problem our system at the  moment is built on the idea that  actually the ministers are the ones who  are going to come in and challenge the  group think the flies in the ointment  they're going to come in and say no no  it may be done like this before we're  going to do it like this now  and everyone's conditioned to obey  ministers but we're not experts and we  don't have that institutional memory  of those fields and so you need  contrary thinking civil servants who  challenge  perceived ways of doing things  a  slightly different topic um you  mentioned ebola  in the course of your your evidence now  ebola can be transmitted by blood could  it not  i wouldn't know but a hemorrhagic fever  yeah but um  how what to what